{"module":"slavery-law","voice":"female","duration":186.2,"segments":[{"el":"title","text":"Slavery makes race law","start":0.0,"end":1.89,"words":[[0.02,0.76],[0.76,1.22],[1.22,1.59],[1.59,1.78]]},{"el":"intro","text":"Slavery in America was not a Southern side story.","start":2.69,"end":6.11,"words":[[2.69,3.28],[3.28,3.42],[3.42,3.94],[3.94,4.16],[4.16,4.38],[4.38,4.52],[4.52,4.89],[4.89,5.18],[5.18,5.62]]},{"el":"intro","text":"It was written into the Constitution, financed by Northern banks, and was the country's largest financial asset by 1860.","start":6.46,"end":14.29,"words":[[6.52,6.65],[6.65,6.85],[6.85,7.19],[7.19,7.46],[7.46,7.6],[7.6,8.48],[8.72,9.26],[9.26,9.47],[9.47,9.8],[9.8,10.21],[10.45,10.65],[10.65,10.86],[10.86,10.99],[10.99,11.47],[11.47,11.94],[11.94,12.55],[12.55,12.82],[12.82,13.03],[13.03,13.84]]},{"el":"h0","text":"The trade","start":14.64,"end":15.43,"words":[[14.69,14.88],[14.88,15.35]]},{"el":"s0","text":"About 12.5 million Africans were forced onto slave ships between the 1500s and 1866, and about 10.7 million survived the Middle Passage (SlaveVoyages.org).","start":16.13,"end":28.64,"words":[[16.18,16.55],[16.55,17.58],[17.58,18.03],[18.03,18.62],[18.62,18.76],[18.76,19.06],[19.06,19.36],[19.36,19.72],[19.72,20.02],[20.02,20.46],[20.46,20.61],[20.61,21.64],[21.64,21.86],[21.86,23.05],[23.3,23.53],[23.53,23.89],[23.89,24.85],[24.85,25.3],[25.3,25.81],[25.81,25.96],[25.96,26.33],[26.33,26.77],[26.77,28.17]]},{"el":"s0","text":"About 388,000 were brought directly to mainland North America.","start":28.99,"end":33.84,"words":[[29.04,29.35],[29.35,31.05],[31.05,31.17],[31.17,31.42],[31.42,31.99],[31.99,32.12],[32.12,32.62],[32.62,32.81],[32.81,33.31]]},{"el":"s0","text":"After Congress banned importing enslaved people in 1808, a domestic trade forcibly moved about a million people from the Upper South to the cotton fields of the Deep South, tearing families apart.","start":34.19,"end":46.73,"words":[[34.24,34.55],[34.55,35.08],[35.08,35.39],[35.39,35.93],[35.93,36.54],[36.54,36.93],[36.93,37.08],[37.08,37.93],[38.2,38.35],[38.35,38.96],[38.96,39.35],[39.35,39.89],[39.89,40.19],[40.19,40.58],[40.58,40.73],[40.73,41.19],[41.19,41.58],[41.58,41.88],[41.88,42.04],[42.04,42.27],[42.27,42.58],[42.58,42.73],[42.73,42.88],[42.88,43.27],[43.27,43.65],[43.65,43.81],[43.81,43.96],[43.96,44.19],[44.19,44.57],[44.84,45.3],[45.3,45.84],[45.84,46.23]]},{"el":"h1","text":"Built into the nation's economy","start":47.63,"end":49.61,"words":[[47.69,47.99],[47.99,48.29],[48.29,48.44],[48.44,48.97],[48.97,49.49]]},{"el":"s1","text":"By 1860, about 3.95 million people were enslaved in the U.S. Their market value as “property” was greater than that of all the country's railroads and factories combined.","start":50.31,"end":61.81,"words":[[50.36,50.58],[50.58,51.43],[51.68,52.04],[52.04,53.18],[53.18,53.6],[53.6,53.96],[53.96,54.1],[54.1,54.67],[54.67,54.81],[54.81,54.95],[54.95,55.31],[55.67,55.88],[55.88,56.24],[56.24,56.59],[56.59,56.73],[56.73,57.23],[57.23,57.45],[57.45,57.87],[57.87,58.09],[58.09,58.3],[58.3,58.44],[58.44,58.59],[58.59,58.73],[58.73,59.23],[59.23,59.87],[59.87,60.08],[60.08,60.65],[60.65,61.29]]},{"el":"s1","text":"Cotton grown by enslaved people made up more than half of U.S. exports.","start":62.16,"end":66.22,"words":[[62.16,62.45],[62.45,62.75],[62.75,62.93],[62.93,63.41],[63.41,63.71],[63.71,63.94],[63.94,64.06],[64.06,64.18],[64.18,64.36],[64.36,64.54],[64.54,64.66],[64.66,64.96],[65.25,65.73]]},{"el":"s1","text":"Northern banks took enslaved people as loan collateral, insurers wrote policies on their lives, and universities profited: in 1838 the Jesuits who ran Georgetown sold 272 people to keep the college afloat.","start":66.57,"end":81.64,"words":[[66.63,67.0],[67.0,67.37],[67.37,67.6],[67.6,68.19],[68.19,68.56],[68.56,68.71],[68.71,69.01],[69.01,69.75],[70.01,70.61],[70.61,70.91],[70.91,71.43],[71.43,71.57],[71.57,71.8],[71.8,72.24],[72.5,72.73],[72.73,73.54],[73.54,74.21],[74.47,74.62],[74.62,75.59],[75.59,75.74],[75.74,76.41],[76.41,76.56],[76.56,76.78],[76.78,77.45],[77.45,77.82],[77.82,79.31],[79.31,79.68],[79.68,79.83],[79.83,80.05],[80.05,80.2],[80.2,80.64],[80.64,81.16]]},{"el":"h2","text":"Written into the law","start":82.54,"end":83.76,"words":[[82.59,83.01],[83.01,83.34],[83.34,83.51],[83.51,83.68]]},{"el":"s2","text":"The Constitution counted enslaved people as three-fifths of a person for representation, protected the slave trade until 1808, and required escaped people to be returned.","start":84.46,"end":96.06,"words":[[84.51,84.66],[84.66,85.56],[85.56,86.08],[86.08,86.68],[86.68,87.05],[87.05,87.2],[87.2,87.8],[87.8,87.95],[87.95,88.1],[88.1,88.47],[88.47,88.62],[88.62,89.74],[90.01,90.68],[90.68,90.83],[90.83,91.2],[91.2,91.58],[91.58,91.95],[91.95,92.77],[93.04,93.26],[93.26,93.86],[93.86,94.38],[94.38,94.76],[94.76,94.91],[94.91,95.06],[95.06,95.58]]},{"el":"s2","text":"The Fugitive Slave Act of 1850 forced free states to help capture them.","start":96.41,"end":101.32,"words":[[96.47,96.61],[96.61,97.27],[97.27,97.63],[97.63,97.85],[97.85,97.99],[97.99,98.72],[98.72,99.01],[99.01,99.23],[99.23,99.66],[99.66,99.81],[99.81,100.1],[100.1,100.53],[100.53,100.82]]},{"el":"s2","text":"In Dred Scott v. Sandford (1857) Chief Justice Taney wrote that Black people had “no rights which the white man was bound to respect.”","start":101.67,"end":111.58,"words":[[101.72,101.88],[101.88,102.2],[102.2,102.52],[102.52,102.76],[103.16,103.71],[103.71,104.91],[105.19,105.51],[105.51,106.07],[106.07,106.47],[106.47,106.79],[106.79,107.03],[107.03,107.35],[107.35,107.74],[107.74,107.98],[107.98,108.22],[108.22,108.62],[108.62,108.94],[108.94,109.1],[109.1,109.42],[109.42,109.66],[109.66,109.9],[109.9,110.3],[110.3,110.46],[110.46,111.1]]},{"el":"h3","text":"Did violence drive cotton's productivity?","start":112.48,"end":115.26,"words":[[112.52,112.72],[112.72,113.25],[113.25,113.58],[113.58,113.97],[113.97,114.83]]},{"el":"s3","text":"Historian Edward Baptist (The Half Has Never Been Told, 2014) argued that rising cotton-picking rates came largely from systematic torture and quotas.","start":115.96,"end":126.98,"words":[[116.01,116.73],[116.73,117.14],[117.14,117.7],[117.7,117.86],[117.86,118.11],[118.11,118.35],[118.35,118.67],[118.67,118.91],[118.91,119.4],[119.68,120.57],[120.85,121.25],[121.25,121.5],[121.5,121.98],[121.98,122.79],[122.79,123.19],[123.19,123.51],[123.51,124.0],[124.0,124.32],[124.32,125.13],[125.13,125.53],[125.53,125.77],[125.77,126.42]]},{"el":"s3","text":"Economists Alan Olmstead and Paul Rhode answered that better cotton varieties explain most of the gain.","start":127.33,"end":133.47,"words":[[127.38,128.09],[128.09,128.37],[128.37,128.87],[128.87,129.09],[129.09,129.3],[129.3,129.59],[129.59,129.94],[129.94,130.16],[130.16,130.44],[130.44,130.8],[130.8,131.44],[131.44,132.01],[132.01,132.36],[132.36,132.51],[132.51,132.65],[132.65,133.01]]},{"el":"s3","text":"Both sides agree enslaved people were whipped, sold and separated from their families; the debate is over how much violence drove productivity.","start":133.82,"end":143.07,"words":[[133.86,134.15],[134.15,134.51],[134.51,134.81],[134.81,135.39],[135.39,135.75],[135.75,135.9],[135.9,136.27],[136.52,136.89],[136.89,137.1],[137.1,137.83],[137.83,138.13],[138.13,138.34],[138.34,138.93],[139.18,139.33],[139.33,139.77],[139.77,139.91],[139.91,140.2],[140.2,140.42],[140.42,140.72],[140.72,141.3],[141.3,141.66],[141.66,142.61]]},{"el":"h4","text":"Resistance and freedom","start":143.97,"end":145.79,"words":[[144.02,144.86],[144.86,145.14],[145.14,145.7]]},{"el":"s4","text":"Enslaved people resisted every day, by slowing work, escaping and rebelling: Stono (1739), the German Coast uprising (1811), Nat Turner (1831).","start":146.49,"end":158.94,"words":[[146.54,147.16],[147.16,147.54],[147.54,148.15],[148.15,148.46],[148.46,148.76],[149.03,149.26],[149.26,149.72],[149.72,150.03],[150.29,150.91],[150.91,151.14],[151.14,151.75],[152.02,152.55],[152.55,153.85],[154.12,154.27],[154.27,154.73],[154.73,155.11],[155.11,155.73],[155.73,156.64],[156.91,157.14],[157.14,157.45],[157.45,158.44]]},{"el":"s4","text":"Harriet Tubman made about 13 trips back into Maryland and led about 70 people to freedom.","start":159.29,"end":165.2,"words":[[159.33,159.74],[159.74,160.15],[160.15,160.42],[160.42,160.77],[160.77,161.11],[161.11,161.45],[161.45,161.66],[161.66,161.93],[161.93,162.55],[162.55,162.75],[162.75,162.96],[162.96,163.3],[163.3,163.78],[163.78,164.12],[164.12,164.26],[164.26,164.73]]},{"el":"s4","text":"Maroon communities lived in places like the Great Dismal Swamp.","start":165.55,"end":169.42,"words":[[165.6,165.92],[165.92,166.56],[166.56,166.81],[166.81,166.94],[166.94,167.39],[167.39,167.65],[167.65,167.77],[167.77,168.09],[168.09,168.48],[168.48,168.86]]},{"el":"s4","text":"About 179,000 Black men served in the Union Army.","start":169.77,"end":174.24,"words":[[169.82,170.15],[170.15,172.15],[172.15,172.41],[172.41,172.61],[172.61,172.88],[172.88,173.01],[173.01,173.15],[173.15,173.55],[173.55,173.81]]},{"el":"s4","text":"On June 19, 1865, Union troops announced emancipation in Galveston, Texas; Juneteenth became a federal holiday in 2021.","start":174.59,"end":185.3,"words":[[174.63,174.78],[174.78,175.06],[175.06,175.64],[175.89,177.03],[177.28,177.71],[177.71,178.07],[178.07,178.57],[178.57,179.42],[179.42,179.57],[179.57,180.28],[180.53,181.03],[181.28,181.92],[181.92,182.35],[182.35,182.5],[182.5,183.0],[183.0,183.5],[183.5,183.64],[183.64,184.78]]}]}